// Binding a CALL RESULT that is a reference into a `let` local has no route.
// The same call returning a scalar binds fine. Found by attributing the macOS
// arm64 native roster, where four fixtures sit at `statement sequence: call:
// flow call` -- `pass/dependent/runtime_sibling_len_index_exit` spells it
// `let view: &[u8] = self.buf.as_slice();`.
//
//   let v: u32   = self.plain();            checks   (scalar result)
//   let v: &u32  = self.borrow_it();        OMITTED  (this fixture)
//   let v: &[u8] = self.buf.as_slice();     OMITTED  (a std method)
//
// A STATEMENT call with a `&mut` argument checks, so it is not calls, and not
// references in argument position -- it is a reference in RESULT position
// reaching a local binding.
//
// SAME GAP as `tests/omega/pending/borrows/reference_local_binding_routes`,
// which found that a reference binding routes ONLY for a `&mut` of a
// self-rooted place named directly. A call result is one more producer that
// does not route. The label is not the cause and the phase varies with the
// PRODUCER, which is why this gap looks like several unrelated clusters:
//
//   `&self.size`, `&self.buf[0]`, `&mut self.buf[off]`, a reference recast
//                       -> `statement sequence: local data: structural call binding`
//   a borrow of a plain local, with a transition following
//                       -> `state graph: result custody accounting`
//   `self.buf.as_slice()`
//                       -> `statement sequence: call: flow call`
//   an own machine returning `&u32`   (this fixture)
//                       -> `state graph: operation custody: reference custody`
//   `&self.buf[0]` in a single-body machine with no transition
//                       -> `statement sequence: unsupported statement kind`
//
// Seven labels, one capability. The last row also shows the phase depends on
// the SURROUNDING program, not only the statement: the identical borrow
// reports a different phase once a transition follows it. Do not split this
// into separate items by label, and do not pin a repair on any of the strings.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
    count: u32;
}

machine Main::borrow_count(&self) -> &u32 {
    transition { _ -> out() }
    state out(&self) -> &u32 { &self.count }
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Console {
    self.count = 3;
    let borrowed: &u32 = self.borrow_count();
    self.console.exit_process(0);
}
